❌ Problems a Senior Developer Would Reject
1. The login is completely fake

dispatch(login({ token: "my-secret-token", email }));
localStorage.setItem("token", "my-secret-token");
The hardcoded "my-secret-token" and the comment "Demo login – any email & password works" means there is zero real authentication. The app/api/auth/ folders are all empty. MongoDB/Mongoose is imported but never called from any API route.

2. The notes API uses in-memory storage

let notes: Note[] = [];
This resets every time the server restarts. In a serverless environment (like Vercel), it resets on almost every request. This is only acceptable for a quick demo, not for a real app.

3. NoteCard.tsx uses inline styles instead of Tailwind The rest of the app uses Tailwind consistently, but NoteCard uses raw style={{}} objects. This is inconsistent. Also, the NoteCard component exists but is never actually used — page.tsx renders the note cards inline instead.

4. AuthGuard.tsx has a logic bug

const commonRoutes = ['/', 'home']  // note: 'home' not '/home'
if (commonRoutes.includes(pathname)) { return; }
'home' should be '/home'. Also, the home page (/) is fully unprotected — anyone can see notes without logging in because it's in commonRoutes. The "Add Note" button is also visible to unauthenticated users even on the home page.

5. useDeleteNote uses id: number but should use string MongoDB uses string _id (ObjectId). Since the DB layer is planned but not connected yet, id: number (using Date.now()) will cause a type mismatch when you eventually wire up MongoDB.

6. addNotePage uses a relative path

router.push("add")  // missing leading slash
This should be router.push("/add"). Relative pushes can break depending on the current URL.

7. 
layout.tsx
 still has the default metadata

title: "Create Next App",
description: "Generated by create next app",
This was never updated — a minor detail, but shows the app isn't finished.

8. Empty folders signal incomplete work contexts/, controllers/, models/, services/, app/signup/, and all app/api/auth/ subfolders are completely empty. This suggests the architecture was planned but never implemented.

Overall Verdict
This is a learning project / prototype, not production-ready code. The folder structure is correct and shows you understand modern Next.js conventions. The Redux and DB caching patterns are solid. But the authentication is fake, the data layer is in-memory, key components are inconsistent or unused, and about half the planned features are missing.

For a senior developer review, the architecture direction is right, but the implementation needs to be completed — real auth API routes, real DB calls, consistent component usage, and the security gaps closed.